#: ../../source/arduino_start/07_basic_variable.rst:16
msgid "Variable"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/arduino_start/07_basic_variable.rst:18
msgid "The variable is one of the most powerful and critical tools in a program. It helps us to store and call data in our programs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/arduino_start/07_basic_variable.rst:20
msgid "The following sketch file uses variables. It stores the pin numbers of the on-board LED in the variable ``ledPin`` and a number \"500\" in the variable ``delayTime``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/arduino_start/07_basic_variable.rst:39
msgid "Wait, is this a duplicate of what ``#define`` does? The answer is NO."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/arduino_start/07_basic_variable.rst:41
msgid "The role of ``#define`` is to simply and directly replace text, it is not considered by the compiler as part of the program."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/arduino_start/07_basic_variable.rst:42
msgid "A ``variable``, on the other hand, exists within the program and is used to store and call value. A variable can also modify its value within the program, something that a define cannot do."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/arduino_start/07_basic_variable.rst:44
msgid "The sketch file below self-adds to the variable and it will cause the on-board LED to blink longer after each blink."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/arduino_start/07_basic_variable.rst:64
msgid "Declare a variable"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/arduino_start/07_basic_variable.rst:66
msgid "Declaring a variable means creating a variable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/arduino_start/07_basic_variable.rst:68
msgid "To declare a variable, you need two things: the data type, and the variable name. The data type needs to be separated from the variable by a space, and the variable declaration needs to be terminated by a ``;``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/arduino_start/07_basic_variable.rst:70
msgid "Let's use this variable as an example."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/arduino_start/07_basic_variable.rst:76
msgid "**Data Type**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/arduino_start/07_basic_variable.rst:78
msgid "Here ``int`` is a data type called integer type, which can be used to store integers from -32768 to 32766. It can also not be used to store decimals."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/arduino_start/07_basic_variable.rst:80
msgid "Variables can hold different kinds of data other than integers. The Arduino language (which, remember, is C++) has built-in support for a few of them (only the most frequently used and useful are listed here):"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/arduino_start/07_basic_variable.rst:82
msgid "``float``: Store a decimal number, for example 3.1415926."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/arduino_start/07_basic_variable.rst:83
msgid "``byte``: Can hold numbers from 0 to 255."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/arduino_start/07_basic_variable.rst:84
msgid "``boolean``: Holds only two possible values, ``True`` or ``False``, even though it occupies a byte in memory."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/arduino_start/07_basic_variable.rst:85
msgid "``char``: Holds a number from -127 to 127. Because it is marked as a ``char`` the compiler will try to match it to a character from the |link_ascii|."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/arduino_start/07_basic_variable.rst:86
msgid "``string``: Can stores a string of characters, e.g. ``Halloween``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/arduino_start/07_basic_variable.rst:89
msgid "**Variable Name**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/arduino_start/07_basic_variable.rst:92
msgid "You can set the variable to any name you want, such as ``i``, ``apple``, ``Bruce``, ``R2D2``, ``Sectumsempra``, but there are some basic rules to follow."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/arduino_start/07_basic_variable.rst:94
msgid "describe what it is used for. Here, I named the variable delayTime, so you can easily understand what it does. It works fine if I name the variable ``barryAllen``, but it confuses the person looking at the code."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/arduino_start/07_basic_variable.rst:96
msgid "Use regular nomenclature. You can use CamelCase like I did, with the initial T in ``delayTime`` so that it is easy to see that the variable consists of two words. Also, you can use UnderScoreCase to write the variable as ``delay_time``. It doesn't affect the program's running, but it would help the programmer to read the code if you use the nomenclature you prefer."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/arduino_start/07_basic_variable.rst:98
msgid "Don't use keywords. Similar to what happens when we type \"int\", the Arduino IDE will color it to remind you that it is a word with a special purpose and cannot be used as a variable name. Change the name of the variable if it is colored."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/arduino_start/07_basic_variable.rst:100
msgid "Special symbols are not allowed. For example, space, #, $, /, +, %, etc. The combination of English letters (case sensitive), underscores, and numbers (but numbers cannot be used as the first character of a variable name) is rich enough."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/arduino_start/07_basic_variable.rst:103
msgid "**Assign a value to a variable**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/arduino_start/07_basic_variable.rst:105
msgid "Once we have declared the variable, it is time to store the data. We use the assignment operator (i.e. ``=``) to put value into the variable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/arduino_start/07_basic_variable.rst:107
msgid "We can assign values to the variable as soon as we declare it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/arduino_start/07_basic_variable.rst:114
msgid "It is also possible to assign a new value to it at some time."
msgstr ""
